1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,600
Twenty one days.

2
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:06,305
Sixty six days. Fifty nine days.

3
00:00:06,685 --> 00:00:11,980
All three of those numbers are real. Each
one comes from an actual book or an actual

4
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:13,210
study.

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:16,265
And not one of them is about you.

6
00:00:16,645 --> 00:00:21,645
Not because you're special. Because of
what those numbers are, underneath.

7
00:00:22,745 --> 00:00:27,345
Start with twenty one, because it's the
oldest and the most wrong.

8
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:33,700
It comes from a book published in nineteen
sixty. Psycho-Cybernetics, by Maxwell

9
00:00:33,772 --> 00:00:34,225
Maltz.

10
00:00:34,605 --> 00:00:39,005
Maltz was a plastic surgeon. He was not
studying habits.

11
00:00:39,385 --> 00:00:44,260
He noticed that his patients took a while
to stop seeing their old face. That

12
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:48,860
amputees sensed a phantom limb for a
stretch before it faded.

13
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:54,940
What he wrote was that it requires a
minimum of about twenty one days for an
old

14
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:58,665
mental image to dissolve and a new one to
jell.

15
00:00:59,045 --> 00:01:04,845
A minimum. About. And it's describing
self-image after surgery, not flossing.

16
00:01:05,225 --> 00:01:10,460
Those qualifiers fell off somewhere in the
decades since. The number kept travelling

17
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:11,825
without them.

18
00:01:12,925 --> 00:01:16,850
Now sixty six, which at least came from a
study.

19
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:24,140
In two thousand ten, Phillippa Lally's
team at University College London followed

20
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:26,105
ninety six people for eighty four days.

21
00:01:26,485 --> 00:01:32,240
Each person picked one daily behaviour and
reported, every single day, how automatic

22
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:33,710
it felt.

23
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:39,700
That last part matters more than the
number. What got measured was not whether
they

24
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,065
finished the task.

25
00:01:41,445 --> 00:01:46,545
It was automaticity. How little
deliberation the action still needed.

26
00:01:46,925 --> 00:01:52,425
Plot that over time and you get a curve
that climbs fast and then flattens out.

27
00:01:52,805 --> 00:01:58,720
Sixty six days is roughly where the
average curve got close to its own
ceiling. It

28
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:01,855
describes a shape. It is not a deadline.

29
00:02:02,955 --> 00:02:07,930
Then two thousand twenty four, and the
biggest look at this so far.

30
00:02:08,310 --> 00:02:15,180
Singh, Murphy, Maher and Smith pulled
together twenty studies covering two
thousand

31
00:02:15,181 --> 00:02:16,010
six hundred and one adults.

32
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:22,240
Here's the first uncomfortable detail. Of
those twenty studies, only four actually

33
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:25,515
reported how long habit formation took.

34
00:02:25,895 --> 00:02:31,195
Four. That's the entire evidence base
under the number everyone repeats.

35
00:02:31,575 --> 00:02:37,200
Among the people who did form a habit, the
median was about fifty nine days.

36
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:42,380
And the range ran from four days to three
hundred and thirty five.

37
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:46,180
Sit with that range for a second.

38
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:50,285
Four days to three hundred and thirty five
days.

39
00:02:50,665 --> 00:02:54,365
That is not a timeline with error bars
around it.

40
00:02:54,745 --> 00:03:00,000
It's a statement that this varies by
almost two orders of magnitude, depending
on

41
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,195
the person and the behaviour.

42
00:03:02,575 --> 00:03:07,740
When a range is that wide, the middle of
it stops being a prediction. It becomes

43
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:09,075
trivia.

44
00:03:10,175 --> 00:03:16,050
And there's a second detail in that same
review, which I think is the important
one.

45
00:03:16,430 --> 00:03:22,140
Only twenty three percent of participants
reached the threshold the researchers had

46
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:23,580
set for a habit.

47
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:26,135
Twenty three percent.

48
00:03:26,515 --> 00:03:31,820
So the median isn't the midpoint of
everyone who tried. It's the midpoint of
the

49
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:33,240
people who got there.

50
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:38,560
That's a survivorship number. You're being
quoted the average time of the finishers

51
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:42,220
and handed it as a forecast for yourself.

52
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:48,650
Nobody is lying to you. The number just
lost its denominator on the way over.

53
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:54,820
Which would be a harmless statistics
complaint, except for what the number does
once

54
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:56,450
it's in your head.

55
00:03:56,830 --> 00:04:01,755
A timeline is a deadline. And a deadline
produces a verdict.

56
00:04:02,135 --> 00:04:07,285
Pick sixty six. Day sixty seven arrives,
and it still takes effort.

57
00:04:07,665 --> 00:04:12,265
You now have evidence. Not about the
behaviour. About yourself.

58
00:04:12,645 --> 00:04:18,440
The honest reading says you're sitting
comfortably inside a range that runs to
three

59
00:04:18,741 --> 00:04:20,845
hundred and thirty five days.

60
00:04:21,225 --> 00:04:25,175
The number in your head says you failed
six weeks ago.

61
00:04:25,555 --> 00:04:31,080
A statistic quietly became a character
assessment, and nobody asked it to.

62
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:34,630
So change what you're asking.

63
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:40,700
Every one of these studies is measuring
the same underlying thing: how automatic
an

64
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,735
action becomes when it's repeated in a
stable context.

65
00:04:45,115 --> 00:04:49,915
The clock is not the input. Repetition in
the same setting is.

66
00:04:50,295 --> 00:04:54,320
Same trigger, same place, same position in
the day.

67
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:57,700
That's the variable you actually hold.

68
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:02,680
Days pass whether or not you do anything.
Repetitions don't.

69
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:09,055
There's a version of this written for
doctors, and it's refreshingly boring.

70
00:05:09,435 --> 00:05:16,420
In two thousand twelve, Gardner, Lally and
Wardle published guidance in the British

71
00:05:16,421 --> 00:05:17,310
Journal of General Practice.

72
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:22,980
Have the patient repeat one chosen
behaviour in the same context until it
becomes

73
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:25,365
automatic and effortless.

74
00:05:25,745 --> 00:05:31,480
And start absurdly small. Their example is
a sedentary person getting off the bus

75
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:35,345
one stop early, instead of walking the
whole route.

76
00:05:35,725 --> 00:05:39,175
There is no timeline anywhere in that
advice.

77
00:05:39,555 --> 00:05:46,700
And here's the limit I owe you. Four
studies is thin. That twenty twenty four
review

78
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:51,580
used narrative synthesis rather than
pooling the numbers, because the designs

79
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:53,255
disagreed too much to average.

80
00:05:53,635 --> 00:05:59,360
So even fifty nine is soft. I'm not
handing you a better number. I'm saying
the

81
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,310
number is the wrong object.

82
00:06:02,410 --> 00:06:04,385
So here's the swap.

83
00:06:04,765 --> 00:06:09,415
Stop counting days. Count repetitions in
the same context.

84
00:06:09,795 --> 00:06:14,920
Write down the cue, not the deadline.
After I pour the coffee. When I close the

85
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:17,995
laptop. Before I take my shoes off.

86
00:06:18,375 --> 00:06:23,600
Then keep a tally of how many times that
cue fired and you went. That number means

87
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:26,725
something. Day forty one does not.

88
00:06:27,105 --> 00:06:32,280
And when it still feels like effort at
week ten, you haven't missed a deadline.

89
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:38,300
You're inside a range that runs to three
hundred and thirty five days, and you're

90
00:06:38,301 --> 00:06:38,935
still in it.

91
00:06:39,315 --> 00:06:44,420
If you want to try it, put your cue in the
comments. Not the goal. The thing that

92
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:46,290
happens right before.
